UID:4b17db0d73e88aa6@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260904T204459Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Looking Ahead: Eligibility-Dedicated Data Fee
 d to Support MaineCare Renewals and Enrollment
DESCRIPTION:Maine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Ma
 ine.gov\nDHHS\n→\nOffice of MaineCare Services (OMS)\n→\nProvider Reso
 urces\n→\n	 \n			Looking Ahead: Eligibility-Dedicated Data Feed to Suppo
 rt MaineCare Renewals and Enrollment\nLooking Ahead: Eligibility-Dedicated
  Data Feed to Support MaineCare Renewals and Enrollment\nSep 04\, 2026\nTw
 o weeks ago\, we\nannounced\ncommunications support for MaineCare partners
  and providers regarding changes the federal government is making to Maine
 Care (Maine’s Medicaid program) eligibility. This message provides an ea
 rly look at planned data-sharing that will help the Maine Department of He
 alth and Human Services (DHHS) verify information needed for MaineCare eli
 gibility. Our goal is to help eligible Mainers keep their coverage and acc
 ess to services with as little disruption as possible.\nNo action is neede
 d now.\nWe are sharing this information early to make providers aware of w
 hat is being planned. We will share more information as plans and timeline
 s are finalized.\nDHHS will continue to use administrative data sources it
  has access to as much as possible to support automatic (“passive”) re
 newals for MaineCare members. Federal changes to eligibility mean we need 
 additional information to help more eligible members maintain their MaineC
 are coverage. This data will help Mainers continue to benefit from MaineCa
 re\, which in turn will help providers minimize the impact of these federa
 l Medicaid cuts on their revenue and on their charity and uncompensated ca
 re.\nClinical Data Will Help People Stay Enrolled in MaineCare\nElectronic
  health record (EHR) systems contain clinical data that Maine and numerous
  other states are planning to use to help\nexempt\nMedicaid applicants and
  members with work-limiting health conditions from Work Requirements. In M
 aine\, this data will\nsupport MaineCare eligibility determination activit
 ies administered by the Office for Family Independence (OFI)\n. DHHS inten
 ds to partner with\nproviders to collect this information th\nhttps://www.
 maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/looking-ahead-eligibility-
 dedicated-data-feed-support-mainecare-renewals-and-enrollment
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/looking-ah
 ead-eligibility-dedicated-data-feed-support-mainecare-renewals-and-enrollm
 ent
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:af813f56aa041161@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260904T204429Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Trump’s Trade War with Canada is Not Maine
 ’s
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nYou know\, last summer I sat down for a cup of coffee at Tim Horton
 s in Caribou with Ron Turcotte\, the legendary jockey who rode Secretariat
  to the Triple Crown in 1973. Ron Turcotte lived across the border in New 
 Brunswick\, Canada\, but he picked up his mail at the U.S. Post Office in 
 Van Buren\, Maine\, and did errands there.\nHis life showcased the reality
  of so many people living along the Maine-Canadian border. Yeah\, there ma
 y be an international border there\, but our communities\, families\, and 
 economies are deeply entwined. And in places like the Saint John Valley\, 
 these connections existed long before the border ever did.\nThroughout Mai
 ne\, the President's trade war has driven up the cost of everything from g
 roceries to cars and trucks and construction materials we need to build mo
 re homes. Because these tariffs are nothing more than taxes on American fa
 milies and businesses.\nNow the President has escalated his trade war\, im
 posing 50 percent tariffs on many Canadian goods. And life is about to get
  more expensive and more complicated for Maine people\, as well as Canadia
 ns.\nIn the Saint John Valley\, about 90 percent of municipal road salt co
 mes from Canada. And now\, because of the President's new taxes\, a major 
 Canadian distributor has announced that it will halt deliveries of salt fo
 r the foreseeable future. It's not unheard of for The County to see snow i
 n late September or early October\, so this isn't some abstract trade war
 —the President's reckless behavior may soon threaten public safety.\nAnd
  the damage to our state from the President's arbitrary and whiplash tarif
 fs is not limited to business with Canada. In 2024\, a developer wanted to
  create 125 new jobs in Jay\, Maine\, by turning that shuttered paper mill
  into an oriented strand board manufacturing facility. But the President's
  tariffs then increased the cost of importing specialized equipment from E
 urope so that developer was forced to scrap hi\nhttp://www.maine.gov/gover
 nor/mills/news/radio_address/trumps-trade-war-canada-not-maines-2026-09-04
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/trumps-trade-wa
 r-canada-not-maines-2026-09-04
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4da6b52a73452dbb@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260904T162111Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] New counsel memo: Standard of review for Righ
 t-to-Sue requests made before 180 days have passed
DESCRIPTION:MEMO \nTO: Kit Thomson Crossman\, Executive Director \nFROM: B
 arbara Archer Hirsch\, Commission Counsel \nColin R. Hurd\, Deputy Commiss
 ion Counsel \nDATE: July 23\, 2025 \nRE: Standard of review for right-to-s
 ue requests made before 180 days have passed \nAs you are aware\, 5 M.R.S.
  § 4612(6)(B)\, enacted in 2023\, delineates the circumstances \nin which
  the Executive Director (“ED”) shall issue a right-to-sue letter (“R
 TS”) to a complainant \nwho requests one before their complaint has aged
  180 days with the Commission. Prior to the \n2023 enactment\, complainant
 s could never request an RTS prior to 180 days. Now\, the \nCommission “
 shall” issue an early RTS when two elements are concurrently met. First\
 , the ED \nmust determine that the complainant demonstrated good cause for
  requesting the RTS before the \npassage of 180 days\, and second\, the ED
  must certify that it is probable that the Commission will \nbe unable to 
 conclude its investigation before the 180\nth day from the date on which t
 he complaint \nwas filed. After 180 days have passed\, the complainant may
  request and receive an RTS without \nhaving to meet these standards.  \nT
 he second element of § 4612(6)(B) is relatively straightforward. The firs
 t element\, \nhowever\, is more nuanced. The Maine Human Rights Act does n
 ot define “good cause”. The \nCommission’s Procedural Rule provides 
 that the Commission may grant extensions of certain \ntime limits “for g
 ood cause shown”\, 94-348 C.M.R. Ch. 2\, § 2.11(C)\, but also do not de
 fine the \nterm.  In practice\, the Commission requires that parties seeki
 ng extensions demonstrate that the \nrequested extension is necessary due 
 to extenuating circumstances\, such as serious personal \nillness or a dea
 th in the family\; inconvenience is insufficient to meet this standard. \n
       \n  \n \n \n  \n \n \n   \n \n   \n   \n \n  \n \n  \n   \n \n   \n 
     \n    \n  \n    \nLooking to other sources\, “good cause” appears 
 in several of the Maine Rules of Civil \nProcedure (“M.R. Civ. P.” or 
 the “Rules”)\, including M.R. Civ. P. 6 (re\nhttps://www.maine.gov/mhr
 c/sites/mhrc/files/inline-files/Standard%20of%20review%20for%20RTS%20reque
 sts%20made%20before%20180%20days.pdf
URL:https://www.maine.gov/mhrc/sites/mhrc/files/inline-files/Standard%20of
 %20review%20for%20RTS%20requests%20made%20before%20180%20days.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e66b5755fdf4546d@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260904T111458Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Housing Trust Fund: Fiscal Year 2026 Allocati
 on Notice
DESCRIPTION:The Housing and Economic Recovery Act of 2008 (HERA) establish
 ed the Housing Trust Fund (HTF) to be administered by HUD. Pursuant to the
  Federal Housing Enterprises Financial Security and Soundness Act of 1992 
 (the Act)\, as amended by HERA\, eligible HTF grantees are the 50 states\,
  the District of Columbia\, the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico\, American Sam
 oa\, Guam\, the Commonwealth of Northern Mariana Islands\, and the United 
 States Virgin Islands. This notice announces the formula allocation amount
  for each eligible HTF grantee.\nhttps://www.federalregister.gov/documents
 /2026/09/04/2026-18163/housing-trust-fund-fiscal-year-2026-allocation-noti
 ce
URL:https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/09/04/2026-18163/housin
 g-trust-fund-fiscal-year-2026-allocation-notice
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
